#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "853bdfff-d043-58d0-bcb7-f1f5891eb31c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5216" :question/problem "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $25x^2 + 9y^2 - 100x - 18y = 116.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-100x-18y & =116 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}-100x)+(9{y}^{2}-18y) & =116 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}-4x]+9[{y}^{2}-2y] & =116 \\\\ 25[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y-1)^{2}-1] & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x-2)^{2}-100+9(y-1)^{2}-9 & =116 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =116+100+9 \\\\ 25(x-2)^{2}+9(y-1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5 > 3,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 5 =10,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 3 =6,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({2},{1}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "0b3df81e-03a7-5cf2-b815-f999012b9b2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5217" :question/problem "Find the center and the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $x^2 + 4y^2 - 6x + 24y + 41 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-6x+24y+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-6x)+(4{y}^{2}+24y)+41 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x)+4[{y}^{2}+6y]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4[(y+3)^{2}-9]+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-9+4(y+3)^{2}-36+41 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =9+36-41 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4(y+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{1} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2 > 1,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2 =4,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 1 =2,$ and the ellipse is centered at $({3},{-3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908681d4-5f25-5951-889f-26e9e13164c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329298} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca17eca-d557-5b5d-971a-cb88d7aeccd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329299} {:answer/id #uuid "673e58c0-cb8b-5547-b18f-d01aa08916b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329300} {:answer/id #uuid "dc532de6-ff41-5bda-82f8-33ff0aa394e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329301} {:answer/id #uuid "b31ecabd-f887-572b-990d-86ebc62eb1eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "908681d4-5f25-5951-889f-26e9e13164c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329298} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c0d1c1-846e-58ea-859c-cd4bd00a45dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8fdb3e7-9493-53f7-a6c7-da3464300fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-115314" :question/problem "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y=-124.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+36x+32y & =-124 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}+36x)+(4{y}^{2}+32y) & =-124 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+12x]+4[{y}^{2}+8y] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+4[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-124 \\\\ 3[(x+6)^{2}-36]+4[(y+4)^{2}-16] & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+6)^{2}-108+4(y+4)^{2}-64 & =-124 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =-124+108+64 \\\\ 3(x+6)^{2}+4(y+4)^{2} & =48 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-6,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc3eb529-1f6a-54d6-8be1-fbbfde367229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329305} {:answer/id #uuid "ae38466b-d76b-5e75-be7c-aec6d761cc2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329306} {:answer/id #uuid "266bc01a-57e7-5e89-bd7f-65cbda81a8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329307} {:answer/id #uuid "f9af7107-23dc-5a0b-9b4f-e7e869f834ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329308} {:answer/id #uuid "72dc653f-89f3-5bc5-be93-71629ad54c9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266bc01a-57e7-5e89-bd7f-65cbda81a8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329307} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b48a5e-0fd7-5158-9e35-2801da6ff289" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "130e116d-8ed6-52b7-9583-d268872d4b07" :question/math-academy-id "q-115316" :question/problem "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is\n$2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+16x-2y+29 & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+16x)+({y}^{2}-2y)+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+8x]+[{y}^{2}-2y]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+[(y-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2[(x+4)^{2}-16]+[(y-1)^{2}-1]+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{2}-32+(y-1)^{2}-1+29 & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =32+1-29 \\\\ 2(x+4)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2164137e-eb8f-5f40-9613-df4b68b4f673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329312} {:answer/id #uuid "d80af9f1-2cae-5892-a22c-6c69d539acfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329313} {:answer/id #uuid "9b080a0f-cc70-56a6-b093-dd9a885dd45c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329314} {:answer/id #uuid "9dba6e70-b115-59d5-a502-b5291257179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329315} {:answer/id #uuid "7fb7c1f4-ef63-5eec-ac52-8f95f410bb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dba6e70-b115-59d5-a502-b5291257179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329315} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec355938-3070-590c-910b-61d40a6cbe69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02554ab2-20a4-5fc6-a60f-c215a86f29de" :question/math-academy-id "q-16561" :question/problem "Determine the length of the minor axis of the ellipse whose equation is  $25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+100x–40y+100 & =0 \\\\ (25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25{x}^{2}+100x)+(4{y}^{2}-40y)+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[{x}^{2}+4x]+4[{y}^{2}-10y]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+4[(y-5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25[(x+2)^{2}-4]+4[(y-5)^{2}-25]+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25(x+2)^{2}-100+4(y-5)^{2}-100+100 & =0 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =-100+100+100 \\\\ 25(x+2)^{2}+4(y-5)^{2} & =100 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5>2,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0dbee47-80f2-5bf5-adbc-53b21d492acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329319} {:answer/id #uuid "a701f455-c658-5b65-a935-93105e26b3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329320} {:answer/id #uuid "018bd471-551c-5e34-a84f-d0fa0c823a96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329321} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eae282-0591-5525-8197-52562abbbb5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329322} {:answer/id #uuid "35f9846e-de5c-557d-b7ac-3b795ac6d314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a701f455-c658-5b65-a935-93105e26b3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329320} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8eb0cc-df85-57cb-b4f1-9171470e0eb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15072ac4-f75c-57bd-98e4-31b68da7b4f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-16562" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(6,-4)$\n- The length of the major axis is $6$\n- The length of the minor axis is $4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}–48x+72y+144 & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-48x)+(9{y}^{2}+72y)+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-12x]+9[{y}^{2}+8y]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}]+9[(y+4)^{2}-{4}^{2}]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4[(x-6)^{2}-36]+9[(y+4)^{2}-16]+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-6)^{2}-144+9(y+4)^{2}-144+144 & =0 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =-144+144+144 \\\\ 4(x-6)^{2}+9(y+4)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 6=12,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(6,-4).$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, statements II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6250a21c-5285-5eab-a621-61f2e4564ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329326} {:answer/id #uuid "4a5f69d6-fdf0-54c6-886a-f13726e62add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329327} {:answer/id #uuid "52df5604-89cf-5bd9-8198-c2a75c05495e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329328} {:answer/id #uuid "a9814a94-f151-5cc0-bca6-271b67f4bf9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329329} {:answer/id #uuid "acee219e-6406-5695-a7ff-314db3675dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52df5604-89cf-5bd9-8198-c2a75c05495e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329328} :answer-field/id #uuid "c321ff3b-c03d-5428-bdac-bd977ce036a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6c3dfa0-4924-5d37-90ea-09a2c58ae62c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16565" :question/problem "Determine the length of the major axis of the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+10x-16y+25 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+10x)+(4{y}^{2}-16y)+25 & =0 \\\\ [{x}^{2}+10x]+4[{y}^{2}-4y]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}]+4[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}]+25 & =0 \\\\ [(x+5)^{2}-25]+4[(y-2)^{2}-4]+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-25+4(y-2)^{2}-16+25 & =0 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =25-25+16 \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d58fba3c-4bbf-5a74-a911-18db101eb632" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given a Homogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3833e520-ea00-54c1-99c9-25ca5599a465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329333} {:answer/id #uuid "c817e8cc-025a-54d7-a621-4ac44d2e900c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329334} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a44228-ea6b-588f-975f-63761feef529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329335} {:answer/id #uuid "b043cc3d-ad3b-5619-bdae-7daf1a21d573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329336} {:answer/id #uuid "6635f610-6d71-5d3f-8151-6a08d542e2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c817e8cc-025a-54d7-a621-4ac44d2e900c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329334} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd92c1f9-fae6-5883-9cc2-7db74b6263de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4b34d91-162d-5e99-b8ce-279507301e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-16639" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse whose equation is $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(0,0)$\n- The length of the major axis is $3$\n- The length of the minor axis is $2\\sqrt{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3>\\sqrt{3},$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 3=6,$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the ellipse is centered at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a3340-41fc-5696-8019-130372fdf579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b7a3340-41fc-5696-8019-130372fdf579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329340} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8edf95a-9a43-5579-83a2-b4923ea3a48e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329339} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4a94b74-8629-5955-a633-816136e55aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4a94b74-8629-5955-a633-816136e55aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329342} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf7da68-b2e1-5f1f-bf22-803ac2314ada" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8785ebc4-cb18-57c0-a373-6234a7edbb89" :question/math-academy-id "q-279719" :question/problem "Determine the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x-4y=-20.$\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x-4y & =-20 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}+12x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}^{2}+12x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+6x)+({y}^{2}-4y) & =-20 \\\\ 2[(x+3)^{2}-9]+[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =-20 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}-18+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+3)^{2}-18+(y-2)^{2}-4 & =-20 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =-20+18+4 \\\\ 2(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{1}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\sqrt{2}>1,$ the length of the major axis  $=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the length of the minor axis  $=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2509930-fb41-5e8a-9e8b-3bd10e6c818d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2509930-fb41-5e8a-9e8b-3bd10e6c818d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6086bdbd-179f-5a28-9100-144742508193" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01011493-6cd4-5622-a783-94fdde12035d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01011493-6cd4-5622-a783-94fdde12035d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329347} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b6f5408-73f8-5bea-8ff5-67cbab6755aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf625522-510b-5214-b5b1-c05f88af5732" :question/math-academy-id "q-279720" :question/problem "Determine the lengths of the major and minor axes of the ellipse whose equation is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-16x+18y=119.$\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-16x+18y & =119 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-16x)+(9{y}^{2}+18y) & =119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4{x}^{2}-16x)+(9{y}^{2}+18y) & =119 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-4x)+9({y}^{2}+2y) & =119 \\\\ 4[(x-2)^{2}-4]+9[(y+1)^{2}-1] & =119 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}-16+9(y+1)^{2}-9 & =119\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-16+9(y+1)^{2}-9 & =119 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+9(y+1)^{2} & =119+16+9 \\\\ 4(x-2)^{2}+9(y+1)^{2} & =144 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6>4,$ the length of the major axis  $=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the length of the minor axis  $=2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f0800d1-163a-50ba-b55d-483d45169160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329350} {:answer/id #uuid "78799a76-f02d-5742-85bc-3785656ee7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329351} {:answer/id #uuid "8d00ecb1-0d31-560c-8fb8-1c69998a9f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329352} {:answer/id #uuid "b218dd06-24b6-509a-b979-9c20acc228f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329353} {:answer/id #uuid "27fa5f16-e347-53a5-a3c4-f4f6ae612e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78799a76-f02d-5742-85bc-3785656ee7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329351} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a44d67-f7b4-56de-abfc-56f9231d6993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d89c8157-fdbf-5abc-9fc7-e08ea29025fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-7632" :question/problem "Determine the center of the ellipse whose equation is $5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ terms and the $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}-10x-36y & =4 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10x)+(9{y}^{2}-36y) & =4 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x]+9[{y}^{2}-4y] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+9[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =4 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1]+9[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(x-1)^{2}-5+9(y-2)^{2}-36 & =4 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =4+5+36 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}+9(y-2)^{2} & =45 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse is centered at $(1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d21d6066-f7c8-5a47-b150-d0ae25d2d60b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Axis Lengths Given an Inhomogeneous Polynomial Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "509c36c9-25ff-5186-8cc4-149768146f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329357} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ea0c7d-446d-50ec-ab8f-5f42863446b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329358} {:answer/id #uuid "0e142be1-47bf-57d8-8644-1024e05909b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329359} {:answer/id #uuid "a96070ff-745d-5d5f-9ed9-f6c3fe9d3d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329360} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5e9908-3a0e-5c6b-b28f-45ca7675a20b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ea0c7d-446d-50ec-ab8f-5f42863446b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329358} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b3adea-5150-5978-81f3-967d46b5391b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd2cdae2-5e26-5778-ab7e-65d79d794e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-7639" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse whose equation is ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y=-11.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Its center is $(1,-3)$\n- The length of the major axis  is $2\\sqrt{2}$\n- The length of the minor axis is $4$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must group the $x$ and $y$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x+12y & =-11 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for both the $x$ and $y$ terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-2x)+(2{y}^{2}+12y) & =-11 \\\\ [{x}^{2}-2x]+2[{y}^{2}+6y] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}]+2[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ [(x-1)^{2}-1]+2[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rearrange the equation into the desired form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}-1+2(y+3)^{2}-18 & =-11 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =-11+1+18 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+2(y+3)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{2}>2,$ the length of the major axis is $2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ the length of the minor axis is $2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the ellipse is centered at $(1,-3).$\n\nTherefore,  I and III are true, and II is false."}]}
